  "rendered": "The strain that reached that prodigiosin titer is R82[1], described in the section on prodigiosin production engineering. It was obtained by adaptive laboratory evolution rather than targeted genetic engineering: the authors evolved the parent strain under high osmotic pressure to select an osmotically tolerant derivative.[2][3][4][5] The parent strain and the number of generations of evolution were: SDSPY-136 → R82[6], evolved for 120 generations[7], with the selection pressure being high osmolarity, summarized in the products table as Adaptive laboratory evolution for improved osmotic tolerance[8]. The fermentation scale stated in the text is a 5 L fed-batch fermentation[9], in which the titer was 11.4 g[10]L^(cid:0) 1 prodigiosin (~double the original strain)[11]. Note that Table 2[12] lists the same titer for this strain, 11,400[13] (in the units of its titer column, (mg L^(cid:0) 1)[14]), but records the cultivation mode as shake flask[15] with a time of 48[16] h, so the document is internally inconsistent on the cultivation format, while agreeing on the titer. Mechanistically, the improvement was attributed to broad transcriptional rewiring that improved precursor supply and product export.[17][18][19] The number of reprogrammed genes reported is 830 genes[20], and the work is cited as reference [18][21]. For comparison, this is the highest prodigiosin titer among the engineered strains listed in that table, computed as the maximum of its prodigiosin titer entries: 15,630[22] mg per litre.",
